pt lat="36.849751" lon="-4.245887"><ele>720.0</ele><name><![CDATA[The Cistern.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[The Cistern.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.850045" lon="-4.245418"><ele>721.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Fortress Ruins]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Fortress Ruins]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.849927" lon="-4.246152"><ele>723.0</ele><name><![CDATA[The Keep.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[The Keep.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.84951" lon="-4.246917"><ele>709.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Typical narrow street.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Typical narrow street.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.84987" lon="-4.247173"><ele>698.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Water Gate.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Water Gate.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.850022" lon="-4.247556"><ele>688.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Construction.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Construction.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.849864" lon="-4.248026"><ele>701.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Beautiful spots.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Beautiful spots.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.849534" lon="-4.248674"><ele>719.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Left uphill]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Left uphill]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.849374" lon="-4.248619"><ele>720.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Alleyways.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Alleyways.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.848975" lon="-4.248777"><ele>740.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Our Lady of the Incarnation Church, 16th Century]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Our Lady of the Incarnation Church, 16th Century]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.848603" lon="-4.24901"><ele>737.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Fountain.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Fountain.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.848096" lon="-4.249278"><ele>744.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Views of Vélez Málaga]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Views of Vélez Málaga]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.848202" lon="-4.248719"><ele>731.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Corners]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Corners]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.848106" lon="-4.248825"><ele>732.0</ele><name><![CDATA[VF El Turrion.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[VF El Turrion.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.848371" lon="-4.24785"><ele>715.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Strategic viewpoints.]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Strategic viewpoints.]]></cmt></wpt><wpt lat="36.848176" lon="-4.247967"><ele>703.0</ele><name><![CDATA[Arab Gate]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[Arab Gate]]></cmt></wpt><trk><name><![CDATA[Visit to Comares (Málaga).]]></name><cmt><![CDATA[This is a walk through the streets of Comares, a beautiful Spanish municipality in the province of Málaga, Andalusia, with the title of Marquisate, located in the foothills of the Montes de Málaga, 703 meters above sea level. Comares is one of the 31 towns that make up the Axarquía region, and is known as the "Balcony of the Axarquía".

There's no need to follow a set route or track. We think it's best to let your instinct guide you. Walk through its beautiful and typical streets, climb the wall, and reach the ruins of its castle.

Discover its beautiful viewpoints and enjoy the views. In other words, enjoy every step.

We had already done the four via ferratas that Comares has, and now it was time to enjoy our strolls through its streets and its history.
A place to enjoy.

The town center is situated on a slight incline, on the crest of a steep mountain, at an altitude of 703 m. It is 25 km from Vélez-Málaga and 39 km from Málaga. The area is full of ravines and farmhouses scattered around the mountain. Its highest point is the Mazmúllar hill (721 m).

The origin of the name Comares is found in the Arabic word Qumaris or Hins Comarix, which means "Castle on the height". But Comares was not founded by the Arabs, but by the Phocaean Greeks who arrived on the coasts of Málaga in the 7th century BC. It must have been a Muslim fortress since the 8th century.

☆As always, remember, when in the mountains, leave no trace that you have been there. Take your trash home!!

NOTICE: The route described here, as well as the track and waypoints, should be considered merely indicative. The tracks do not always mark the exact itinerary, therefore they may contain errors. This is because during GPS recording, the reception of satellites can be weak, especially in areas of ravines, deep gorges, dense forest areas, etc. It is the responsibility of those who carry them out to take the appropriate safety measures for each difficulty.]]></cmt><desc><![CDATA[This is a walk through the streets of Comares, a beautiful Spanish municipality in the province of Málaga, Andalusia, with the title of Marquisate, located in the foothills of the Montes de Málaga, 703 meters above sea level. Comares is one of the 31 towns that make up the Axarquía region, and is known as the "Balcony of the Axarquía".

There's no need to follow a set route or track. We think it's best to let your instinct guide you. Walk through its beautiful and typical streets, climb the wall, and reach the ruins of its castle.

Discover its beautiful viewpoints and enjoy the views. In other words, enjoy every step.

We had already done the four via ferratas that Comares has, and now it was time to enjoy our strolls through its streets and its history.
A place to enjoy.

The town center is situated on a slight incline, on the crest of a steep mountain, at an altitude of 703 m. It is 25 km from Vélez-Málaga and 39 km from Málaga. The area is full of ravines and farmhouses scattered around the mountain. Its highest point is the Mazmúllar hill (721 m).

The origin of the name Comares is found in the Arabic word Qumaris or Hins Comarix, which means "Castle on the height". But Comares was not founded by the Arabs, but by the Phocaean Greeks who arrived on the coasts of Málaga in the 7th century BC. It must have been a Muslim fortress since the 8th century.

☆As always, remember, when in the mountains, leave no trace that you have been there. Take your trash home!!

NOTICE: The route described here, as well as the track and waypoints, should be considered merely indicative. The tracks do not always mark the exact itinerary, therefore they may contain errors. This is because during GPS recording, the reception of satellites can be weak, especially in areas of ravines, deep gorges, dense forest areas, etc. It is the responsibility of those who carry them out to take the appropriate safety measures for each difficulty.]]></desc><trkseg><trkpt lat="36.848053" lon="-4.247916"><ele>702.052</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:49:01Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848066" lon="-4.247973"><ele>703.453</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:49:08Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848105000000004" lon="-4.247914"><ele>702.508</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:49:23Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848124000000006" lon="-4.247856"><ele>703.409</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:49:40Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.84815700000001" lon="-4.2478169999999995"><ele>703.746</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:49:54Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848166000000006" lon="-4.247757999999999"><ele>703.691</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:50:01Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848181000000004" lon="-4.247703"><ele>703.680</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:50:06Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848209000000004" lon="-4.2476449999999994"><ele>703.764</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:50:12Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848246" lon="-4.247593999999999"><ele>700.587</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:50:46Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848293000000005" lon="-4.2476069999999995"><ele>699.686</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:51:23Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848338000000005" lon="-4.247574999999999"><ele>699.773</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:51:43Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848375000000004" lon="-4.247540999999999"><ele>699.773</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:51:49Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848412" lon="-4.247506999999999"><ele>699.782</ele><time>2024-04-02T14:51:55Z</time></trkpt><trkpt lat="36.848453000000006" lon="-4.2474669999999985"><ele>699.727</ele><time>2024-04-02T14